://raw.githubusercontent.com/Theviki20110/unraid-community-template/main/icon.png";s:8:"Overview";s:1309:"feedbard turns the blogs you follow into an audiobook library.

    It polls a list of RSS/Atom feeds, fetches each new post's full article
    (Substack API, the feed's own content, or Readability over the page,
    whichever works), translates it into the language you pick, describes the
    figures and charts so they are narrated instead of skipped, renders the
    result to speech, and publishes it as an Audiobookshelf *book* library:
    one folder per article, per-article cover art, ID3-tagged MP3.

    There is no WebUI. The container runs the pipeline every
    CRON_INTERVAL_SECONDS (default 3 hours) and logs what it did. Posts
    already processed are recorded in a SQLite database under the data path,
    so a restart never re-narrates the same article.

    Two paths, kept apart on purpose:
      /app/data    the pipeline's own state - database, rendered episodes,
                   covers, and resumable per-block scratch files.
      /app/library the published tree. Point Audiobookshelf at this one as a
                   Book library; it never sees scratch files.

    You supply the model backends. LLM: Anthropic API, AWS Bedrock, or a local
    Ollama server. Text-to-speech: an HTTP TTS service or
    Amazon Polly. The feed list is a newline-separated text file you mount in.";s:7:"Support";s:47:"https://github.com/Theviki20110/feedbard/issues";s:7:"Project";s:40:"https://github.com/Theviki20110/feedbard";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:100:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Theviki20110/unraid-community-template/main/templates/feedbard.xml";s:6:"ReadMe";s:70:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Theviki20110/feedbard/main/README.md";s:16:"ExtraSearchTerms";s:81:"rss atom feed podcast tts text-to-speech audiobookshelf narration translation llm";s:8:"Requires";s:372:"<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Internet access. An LLM backend (Anthropic API key, AWS Bedrock<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;credentials, or an Ollama server) and a TTS backend (HTTP TTS service,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;SageMaker endpoint, or Amazon Polly). An Audiobookshelf install pointed at<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;the library path is recommended but not required.<br>";s:4:"Date";s:10:"2026-09-18";s:6:"MinVer";s:5:"6.9.0";s:7:"License";s:3:"MIT";s:6:"Config";a:23:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"Data";s:6:"Target";s:9:"/app/data";s:7:"Default";s:26:"/mnt/user/appdata/feedbard";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:161:"Pipeline state: processed-posts database, rendered episodes, cover art, and resumable scratch shards. The database here is the only state that cannot be rebuilt.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"Library";s:6:"Target";s:12:"/app/library";s:7:"Default";s:35:"/mnt/user/media/audiobooks/feedbard";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:119:"Published Audiobookshelf book library ({Author}/{Title}/). Add this folder in Audiobookshelf as a library of type Book.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Feeds list";s:6:"Tar