Every other sync tool's main screen is a progress bar, which is a report on a decision somebody already made for you. Here you get the decision: every proposed change listed with its direction and the reason for it, each row unticked if you disagree, and nothing touched until you press the button.

It keeps a per-file record of what both sides looked like the last time they agreed, which is what lets it tell "created over here" apart from "deleted over there" instead of guessing. Deletions go to a trash rather than away. A run that would delete an implausible share of your files stops and says so. A side that lists nothing at all is never believed, because a disk that failed to mount looks exactly like a folder somebody emptied.

Local folders, Unraid shares, SMB, SFTP, S3-compatible storage, WebDAV clouds such as Nextcloud and OpenCloud, and the rest of what rclone speaks, compiled in rather than shelled out to.

Set a password for the WebUI under Settings, Security, where two-factor authentication and passkeys live too. Until then anyone who can reach it can drive it, so keep it on your LAN.";s:5:"WebUI";s:24:"http://[IP]:[PORT:8422]/";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:91:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/arrowloop/arrowloop.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:86:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/arrowloop/icon.png";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:45:"--restart unless-stopped --hostname=arrowloop";s:6:"Config";a:7:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"WebUI Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"8422";s:7:"Default";s:4:"8422";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:185:"The port the interface listens on. Until a password is set under Settings, Security, anyone who can reach it can start a sync that deletes files, so do not forward it from the internet.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"8422";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:6:"Config";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:27:"/mnt/user/appdata/arrowloop";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:225:"Holds arrowloop.json, one state database per job and the run log. This is the directory that must survive the container: without the state databases every job forgets what the two sides agreed on and treats every file as new.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:27:"/mnt/user/appdata/arrowloop";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"Data";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Default";s:9:"/mnt/user";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:171:"The shares you want to synchronise. Point job paths at subfolders of this, for example /data/Photos. Mount it read-write: a two-way job writes to both sides by definition.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:9:"/mnt/user";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Password hash";s:6:"Target";s:23:"ARROWLOOP_PASSWORD_HASH";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:501:"Optional. The password is normally set in the interface, under Settings, Security, which also offers two-factor authentication and passkeys. A hash here wins over that password and cannot be changed from the interface, which makes it the way back in after a forgotten one: open this container's console, run: arrowloop hash-password, type the password twice and paste the line it prints here. Neither lives in arrowloop.json, because that file can be downloaded and restored from inside the interface.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:8:"Timezone";s:6:"Target";s:2:"TZ";s:7:"Default";s:13:"Europe/Berlin";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:114:"Used for the schedules. A cron expression means nothing until the container agrees with you about what time it is.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:13:"Europe/Berlin";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Listen address";s:6:"Target";s:14:"ARROWLOOP_ADDR";s:7:"Default";s:12:"0.0.0.0:8422";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:255:"Where the interface binds inside the container. Leave this alone unless you know why you are changing it: outside a container the program defaults to loopback, and the container overrides that because loopback inside a container means nobody can reach it.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:12:"0.0.0.0:8422";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:20:"File permission mask";s:6:"Target";s:15:"ARROWLOOP_UMASK";s:7:"Default";s:3:"000";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:459:"The umask for everything this container writes. It runs as root, so with the usual 022 every copied file would land as 0644 root:root and every folder as 0755 root:root, which you can read over SMB and not change. An Unraid share is 0777 nobody:users, and 000 makes what ArrowLoop writes match it. The field must not be left empty: a blank template field overrides the image default rather than falling back to it. Set 022 to get restrictive permissions back.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:3:"000";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:43:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/arrowloop/arrowloop.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:6:"Backup";i:1;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";}s:9:"downloads";i:6448;s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1790645917;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1790620493;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1790645917;s:8:"Category";s:23:"Backup: Tools:Utilities";s:2:"ID";i:2763;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:9:"arrowloop";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:8:"SortName";s:9:"ArrowLoop";s:6:"random";i:1360128353;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:128:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/junkerderprovinz-ArrowLoop-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2764;a:45:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"BombVault";s:10:"Repository";s:33:"junkerderprovinz/bombvault:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:51:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/bombvault";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:4:"bash";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:74:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/199509-support-junkerderprovinz-bombvault/";s:7:"Project";s:45:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/bombvault";s:6:"ReadMe";s:65:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/bombvault/blob/main/README.md";s:8:"Overview";s:991:"The best and only backup tool you'll ever need for Unraid.

One-click backup and full disaster recovery for your Docker containers, VMs, the flash USB, ZFS datasets and any folders you point it at, powered by restic.

The restore is the star: a backed-up container or VM reappears in the Docker or VM tab exactly as before, with no reinstall and no reconfiguration. Incremental, deduplicated and always encrypted, with off-site repos (including immutable, ransomware-resistant append-only copies with tamper verification), retention, file-level restore, restore from another BombVault server, live restore progress and cancel, integrity checks and off-site DR drills, scheduling and pre/post-backup hooks, all configured in the WebUI. A single "Backup Everything" pass covers containers, VMs, flash, folders, ZFS datasets and BombVault's own config in one run, so one ping afterwards tells you the whole server is protected.

Full documentation: https://junkerderprovinz.github.io/bombvault/";s:5:"WebUI";s:25:"https://[IP]:[PORT:3443]/";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:94:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/bombvault/my-BombVault.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:93:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/bombvault/main/.github/assets/icon.png?v=6";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:90:"--restart unless-stopped --hostname=bombvault --add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway";s:6:"Config";a:14:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"WebUI Port (HTTPS)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3443";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3443";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:88:"The BombVault WebUI (HTTPS, self-signed cert out of the box). This is the port you open.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3443";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"WebUI Port (HTTP)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3000";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3000";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:135:"Plain-HTTP port. Only used when HTTP_ONLY is set to true (i.e. behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy). Leave at the default otherwise.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3000";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"HTTP_ONLY";s:6:"Target";s:9:"HTTP_ONLY";s:7:"Default";s:10:"false|true";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:295:"Serve plain HTTP instead of HTTPS — only for use behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy. false = HTTPS (recommended); true = plain HTTP on the HTTP port above. NOTE: when true, the WebUI link still points to HTTPS — open http://[IP]:[PORT:3000]/ instead (or set the container's WebUI to it).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"APP_KEY";s:6:"Target";s:7:"APP_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:160:"REQUIRED. 64 lowercase hex chars: openssl rand -hex 32. Master key for secrets and the derived restic password; losing it makes encrypted backups unrecoverable.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:6:"Config";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:27:"/mnt/user/appdata/bombvault";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:58:"BombVault's own state (SQLite DB, TLS cert, restic cache).";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:27:"/mnt/user/appdata/bombvault";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"Host Data";s:6:"Target";s:10:"/host/user";s:7:"Default";s:4:"/mnt";s:4:"Mode";s:8:"rw,slave";s:11:"Description";s:562:"Your Unraid storage root /mnt, which covers the user share and the cache and pool mounts, so any container's appdata is reachable wherever it lives. Backup sources and destinations both live here; pick the exact subpaths in the WebUI under Settings. Read for backup, written on restore. Read/Write - Slave, so a share or disk that mounts after the container starts (e.g. /mnt/remotes at boot) becomes visible without restarting BombVault. Keep the Access Mode at Read/Write - Slave: ZFS dataset backups read snapshots that the host mounts after BombVault starts.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"/mnt";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:20:"Unraid Flash (/boot)";s:6:"Target";s:10:"/host/boot";s:7:"Default";s:5:"/boot";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:696:"The Unraid USB flash, mounted whole. Powers two things: (1) Flash backup (Settings - Domains - Flash) of the entire /boot - Unraid OS, license, array config, shares, network and plugin config; and (2) capturing/restoring a container's Unraid template (under /boot/config/plugins/dockerMan/templates-user) so a restored container reappears as a NORMAL, editable Unraid app instead of a 'third-party' container. Leave at the default. Flash RESTORE never overwrites the live flash - it streams the snapshot to your browser as a ready-to-use .zip for the Unraid USB creator (or any fresh USB). The one exception is restoring a single plugin, which writes only that plugin's files back into the flash.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"/boot";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Docker Socket";s:6:"Target";s:20:"/var/run/docker.sock";s:7:"Default";s:20:"/var/run/docker.sock";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:226:"Root-equivalent host control: stop/start/recreate containers around backup/restore. Together with the broad /mnt mount this gives BombVault root-equivalent access to your host, so run it only on a trusted, non-exposed network.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:20:"/var/run/docker.sock";}i:8;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"HOST_SOURCE_ROOT";s:6:"Target";s:16:"HOST_SOURCE_ROOT";s:7:"Default";s:4:"/mnt";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:227:"The HOST path mounted as 'Host Data' (default /mnt). BombVault translates the bind-mount sources Docker reports (e.g. /mnt/user/appdata/x) into paths under this mount. Change only if you mounted a different host root than /mnt.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"/mnt";}i:9;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:24:"BOMBVAULT_SELF_CONTAINER";s:6:"Target";s:24:"BOMBVAULT_SELF_CONTAINER";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:127:"The name of this container, so BombVault never backs up and stops itself. Leave it empty and BombVault finds its own container.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:10;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"BACKUP_MAX_HOURS";s:6:"Target";s:16:"BACKUP_MAX_HOURS";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:340:"Maximum wall-clock hours a single backup run may hold its domain lock before it is force-cancelled (a guard so a wedged run cannot block the domain forever). Leave empty for the 48h default. Raise it for very large or slow cloud backups (a run cancelled at the cap fails with 'context deadline exceeded'). Set 0 to disable the cap entirely.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:11;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"Host SSH: Address";s:6:"Target";s:12:"LIBVIRT_HOST";s:7:"Default";s:11:"192.168.x.x";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:221:"Used by VM backups and ZFS dataset backups to run commands on this server over SSH. Leave the placeholder to use the server itself through host.docker.internal. On a custom network (br0), set this server's LAN IP instead.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:11:"192.168.x.x";}i:12;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Host SSH: Port";s:6:"Target";s:16:"LIBVIRT_SSH_PORT";s:7:"Default";s:2:"22";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:36:"SSH port of this server, usually 22.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:2:"22";}i:13;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Host SSH: User";s:6:"Target";s:16:"LIBVIRT_SSH_USER";s:7:"Default";s:4:"root";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:23:"SSH user, usually root.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"root";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:46:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/bombvault/my-BombVault.xml";s:4:"Re