der over /var/log/euro-office hides that tree, nginx then cannot open its error log and fails with 'Starting nginx nginx ...fail!', and because the entrypoint stops on that error the container crash-loops on every boot. You lose nothing by leaving this blank: the Unraid log button still shows everything the server prints. Only set a path here if it already contains the log tree copied out of a running container first.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:8;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:8:"Database";s:6:"Target";s:19:"/var/lib/postgresql";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:613:"Leave BLANK for a normal install. The image ships with an already-initialised database baked in; mounting an EMPTY folder here hides that database and the container fails to start with 'PostgreSQL ... is not accessible or does not exist' on every boot (an upstream bug: the entrypoint cannot initialise a database in a fresh empty volume - see euro-office/documentserver#299). Without this mount the DB simply resets on container recreate, which is fine for WOPI use with OpenCloud. Only set a path here if it already contains a working euro-office Postgres data directory copied out of a running container first.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:9;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:12:"Certificates";s:6:"Target";s:6:"/certs";s:7:"Default";s:35:"/mnt/user/appdata/euro-office/certs";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"ro";s:11:"Description";s:271:"Optional. Folder for your own TLS certificate and private key, used by the two fields below. Drop cert.pem and key.pem into /mnt/user/appdata/euro-office/certs on the server, then set the fields to /certs/cert.pem and /certs/key.pem. An empty folder here changes nothing.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:35:"/mnt/user/appdata/euro-office/certs";}i:10;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"TLS certificate";s:6:"Target";s:20:"SSL_CERTIFICATE_PATH";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:392:"Leave blank to use the certificate Euro Office creates for itself. For your own, enter its path inside the container, e.g. /certs/cert.pem (not the /mnt/user/... path). Unraid's combined *_unraid_bundle.pem holds certificate and key in one file: enter that same file here and in 'TLS private key'. If either path is wrong, Euro Office falls back to its own certificate and says so in the log.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:11;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"TLS private key";s:6:"Target";s:12:"SSL_KEY_PATH";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:256:"Leave blank to use the certificate Euro Office creates for itself. Otherwise the path inside the container to the key that belongs to 'TLS certificate', e.g. /certs/key.pem. The key must not be passphrase-protected (create it with openssl's -nodes option).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:12;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"Send HSTS header";s:6:"Target";s:29:"ONLYOFFICE_HTTPS_HSTS_ENABLED";s:7:"Default";s:10:"true|false";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:380:"'true' (the default) tells browsers to use https for this host for a year. It only matters with your own trusted certificate and a hostname: browsers ignore it for IP addresses and for the self-signed certificate. Set 'false' if that hostname also serves things you reach over plain http, e.g. unraid.example.com, because the rule applies to the whole hostname regardless of port.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"true";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:47:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/euro-office/euro-office.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:12:"Productivity";i:1;s:5:"Cloud";}s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1787185369;s:8:"Category";s:20:"Productivity: Cloud:";s:2:"ID";i:2768;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:11:"euro-office";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:8:"SortName";s:11:"Euro Office";s:6:"random";i:1768443035;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:129:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/junkerderprovinz-EuroOffice-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2769;a:39:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Excalidraw";s:10:"Repository";s:34:"junkerderprovinz/excalidraw:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:52:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/excalidraw";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:6:"ReadMe";s:66:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/excalidraw/blob/main/README.md";s:7:"Support";s:76:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/198811-support-junkerderprovinz-unraid-apps/";s:7:"Project";s:22:"https://excalidraw.com";s:8:"Overview";s:1842:"Excalidraw is a virtual whiteboard for sketches that look hand-drawn. This is a self-hosted build of it that keeps to itself: shared links, live collaboration, the session scene, the fonts and the icons all come from your own server, and the browser never contacts anyone else.

What that means in practice:
• Shared links are stored in this container, not on json.excalidraw.com
• A live session keeps its scene here, not in Google Firestore
• Pasted images stay here, not in Firebase Storage
• The collaboration socket is this container, not oss-collab.excalidraw.com
• Fonts are served from the image, and the analytics script is gone
• Everything is encrypted in your browser before it is stored. The key sits in the part of the link after the #, which browsers never send to a server, so the container holds bytes it cannot read

Two features would still talk to Excalidraw's servers, so they are switches rather than a decision made for you, and both are off by default: the shape library and the text-to-diagram feature. Off means the request never leaves your server.

Notes:
• Use the HTTPS port. Live collaboration needs crypto.subtle, which browsers only provide over HTTPS, so on the plain HTTP port the "start session" button fails with a cryptography error. The container generates a self-signed certificate on first start and keeps it in /config, so your browser only has to be told once.
• The HTTP port is there for a reverse proxy that terminates TLS, and for the single-user case where no session is ever started.
• Behind a reverse proxy: allow WebSocket upgrades on /socket.io/ and a body size of 64 MB, otherwise collaboration cannot connect and drawings with images are refused.
• /config holds the drawings database and the certificate. Back it up and you have backed up everything.";s:5:"WebUI";s:24:"https://[IP]:[PORT:443]/";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:93:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/excalidraw/excalidraw.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:91:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/excalidraw/icon.png?v=2";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:24:"--restart=unless-stopped";s:6:"Config";a:6:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"WebUI Port (HTTPS)";s:6:"Target";s:3:"443";s:7:"Default";s:4:"8443";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:262:"The port to open in your browser. HTTPS is required for live collaboration, because browsers only provide the cryptography it needs over a secure connection. The certificate is self-signed and generated on first start, so your browser asks you to accept it once.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"8443";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"WebUI Port (HTTP)";s:6:"Target";s:2:"80";s:7:"Default";s:4:"8080";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:255:"For a reverse proxy that terminates TLS in front of the container, or for solo drawing where no session is ever started. Opened directly in a browser, this port cannot run live collaboration, because the cryptography it needs is only available over HTTPS.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"8080";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"AppData (/config)";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:28:"/mnt/user/appdata/excalidraw";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:146:"Holds the drawings database (shared links, live sessions, pasted images) and the self-signed certificate. This is the only thing worth backing up.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:28:"/mnt/user/appdata/excalidraw";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Shape library";s:6:"Target";s:14:"ENABLE_LIBRARY";s:7:"Default";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:301:"Set to true to allow the shape library to load from libraries.excalidraw.com. That is a public catalogue of drawings other people published, so nothing of yours is sent, but your browser does contact Excalidraw's server. Left false, the library button reports an error and nothing leaves your network.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Text to diagram";s:6:"Target";s:9:"ENABLE_AI";s:7:"Default";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:171:"Set to true to allow the text-to-diagram feature, which sends what you typed to Excalidraw's AI service. Left false, the feature reports an error and your text stays here.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"Time zone";s:6:"Target";s:2:"TZ";s:7:"Default";s:13:"Europe/Berlin";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:37:"Time zone used for the container log.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:13:"Europe/Berlin";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:45:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/excalidraw/excalidraw.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:12:"Productivity";i:1;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";}s:9:"downloads";i:5221;s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1788581172;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1790962648;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1791198847;s:8:"Category";s:29:"Productivity: Tools:Utilities";s:2:"ID";i:2769;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:10:"excalidraw";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:8:"SortName";s:10:"Excalidraw";s:6:"random";i:1455300347;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:129:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/junkerderprovinz-Excalidraw-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2770;a:39:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"featherdrop";s:10:"Repository";s:35:"junkerderprovinz/featherdrop:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:53:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/featherdrop";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:4:"bash";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:76:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/199203-support-junkerderprovinz-featherdrop/";s:7:"Project";s:47:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/featherdrop";s:6:"ReadMe";s:67:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/featherdrop/blob/main/README.md";s:8:"Overview";s:761:"featherdrop is a lightweight self-hosted file sharer, a private WeTransfer without accounts or size paywalls. Open the page, drop a file, set an expiry (plus an optional password or download limit), and share a short link or QR code. Files are end-to-end encrypted in your browser before upload, so the server only stores ciphertext and never sees your files, their names or the key. Uploads are resumable (tus), files sit on a local volume and the metadata lives in a single SQLite file, so there is no separate database, no account system and no tracking. The UI comes in 26 languages with light and dark mode, and you can brand it with APP_NAME, APP_LOGO and ACCENT_COLOR. Put it behind a reverse proxy and set BASE_URL so share links use your public domain.";s:5:"WebUI";s:24:"http://[IP]:[PORT:3000]/";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:95:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/featherdrop/featherdrop.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:92:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/featherdrop/icon.png?v=1";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:24:"--restart unless-stopped";s:6:"Config";a:15:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"WebUI Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3000";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3000";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:46:"Port the featherdrop web interface listens on.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3000";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:24:"Data Directory (uploads)";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Default";s:34:"/mnt/user/appdata/featherdrop/data";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:141:"Bulk storage for the uploaded files. Can live on slower array storage. Kept separate from the small metadata database (see Config Directory).";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:34:"/mnt/user/appdata/featherdrop/data";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:27:"Config Directory (database)";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:36:"/mnt/user/appdata/featherdrop/config";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:163:"Stores the small SQLite metadata database (db.sqlite). Best kept on fast storage (e.g. the SSD cache/appdata). Separate from the bulk uploads (see Data Directory).";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:36:"/mnt/user/appdata/featherdrop/config";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:27:"Config Dir (container path)";s:6:"Target";s:10:"CONFIG_DIR";s:7:"Default";s:7:"/config";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:128:"Container path the SQLite database is written to. Must match the Config Directory mount above (/config), so leave it as /config.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:7:"/config";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Public Base URL";s:6:"Target";s:8:"BASE_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:136:"Public base URL for share links behind a reverse proxy, e.g. https://featherdrop.yourdomain.tld. Leave empty to use the browser address.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Default Expiry";s:6:"Target";s:14:"DEFAULT_EXPIRY";s:7:"Default";s:21:"7d|1h|6h|1d|30d|never";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:122:"Expiry pre-selected in the UI when an uploader does not choose one. Pick from the dropdown: 1h, 6h, 1d, 7d, 30d, or never.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:2:"7d";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:21:"Max File Size (bytes)";s:6:"Target";s:13:"MAX_FILE_SIZE";s:7:"Default";s:1:"0";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:101:"Maximum upload size in bytes. 0 = unlimited (only limited by disk space). Example: 5368709120 = 5 GB.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:1:"0";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Max Expiry";s:6:"Target";s:10:"MAX_EXPIRY";s:7:"Default";s:21:"never|1h|6h|1d|7d|30d";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:229:"Longest expiry an uploader may pick. 'never' (default) = no cap. Pick a shorter value to cap it; longer options (incl. 'never') then disappear from the UI and the server rejects them, and the Default Expiry is clamped to the cap.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"never";}i:8;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:21:"Storage Quota (bytes)";s:6:"Target";s:13:"STORAGE_QUOTA";s:7:"Default";s:1:"0";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:180:"Optional. Maximum combined size of all stored shares, in bytes. 0 = unlimited. Uploads that would exceed the quota are rejected before any byte lands. Example: 53687091200 = 50 GB.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:1:"0";}i:9;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Rate Limit";s:6:"Target";s:10:"RATE_LIMIT";s:7:"Default";s:10:"true|false";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:144:"Built-in per-IP rate limiting on uploads and download/password attempts (HTTP 429). Leave on unless you run your own limiter in a reverse proxy.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"true";}i:10;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"Trust Proxy";s:6:"Target";s:11:"TRUST_PROXY";s:7:"Default";s:10:"false|true";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:269:"Set true only when featherdrop runs behind a reverse proxy: rate limits then use the client address from X-Forwarded-For. Leave it false on a directly reachable instance, because there any client could send its own X-Forwarded-For header and get around the rate limits.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:11;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Custom App Name";s:6:"Target";s:8:"APP_NAME";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:160:"Optional. Renames the app by replacing the 'featherdrop' wordmark in the header and the browser tab title with your own name. Leave empty to keep 'featherdrop'.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:12;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Custom Logo URL";s:6:"Target";s:8:"APP_LOGO";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:303:"Optional. Your own logo (SVG/PNG) to replace the feather: either a public image URL, or a data: URI so you need no hosting and no file on disk. To make a data URI, run 'base64 -w0 logo.svg' and prefix the output with data:image/svg+xml;base64, (use image/png for a PNG). Leave empty to keep the feather.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:13;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:12:"Accent Color";s:6:"Target";s:12:"ACCENT_COLOR";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:133:"Optional. A 6-digit hex colour (e.g. #3daee9) for buttons, the upload ring and other accents. Leave empty to keep the gold (#d4af37).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:14;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Upload Password";s:6:"Target";s:15:"UPLOAD_PASSWORD";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:230:"Optional. If set, only people who know this password can upload / create shares; download links still work for everyone (instance-private uploads). Leave empty to allow open uploads. Use a value without leading or trailing spaces.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:47:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/featherdrop/featherdrop.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";i:1;s:12:"Productivity";}s:9:"downloads";i:13636;s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1781028981;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1790378757;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1791745164;s:8:"Category";s:29:"Tools:Utilities Productivity:";s:2:"ID";i:2770;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:11:"featherdrop";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:8:"SortName";s:11:"featherdrop";s:6:"random";i:963667945;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:130:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/junkerderprovinz-featherdrop-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2771;a:36:{s:6:"Plugin";b:1;s:9:"PluginURL";s:88:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire/main/plugin/firesquire.plg";s:12:"PluginAuthor";s:18:"Junker der Provinz";s:4:"Name";s:10:"FireSquire";s:8:"Overview";s:840:"Reads the beacon before your reboot catches fire.

FireSquire checks, BEFORE you reboot, whether your server will come back up clean - and gives one verdict: GO / CAUTION / NO-GO, with the exact findings. Advisory only: it reads the host and reports, it never changes anything.

It catches the reboot landmines that are detectable from the running system: an unclean array or a disabled/invalid disk, a parity/sync/rebuild/clear or mover in progress, a container mounting a host runtime directory or a stuck docker.img/libvirt.img loop, a non-writable flash, low free space, recent kernel/IO errors, running VMs, stopped core services, and SMART health (status + attributes + temperature).

Adds a button next to Reboot on the Main tab. Follows your Unraid light/dark theme and language (all 26 supported languages, English fallback).";s:7:"Support";s:75:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/199508-support-junkerderprovinz-firesquire/";s:7:"Project";s:46:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire";s:4:"Icon";s:94:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire/main/.github/assets/icon.png?v=3";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:93:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/firesquire/firesquire.xml";s:12:"TemplatePath";s:45:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/firesquire/firesquire.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"Repository";s:88:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire/main/plugin/firesquire.plg";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:12:"Tools-System";i:1;s:7:"Plugins";}s:13:"pluginVersion";s:5:"3.0.1";s:6:"MinVer";s:6:"6.10.0";s:6:"Author";s:18:"Junker der Provinz";s:16:"ChangeLogPresent";s:1:"1";s:9:"downloads";i:161;s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1786406663;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1792411842;s:11:"pluginStats";a:13:{i:10;i:9;i:11;i:0;i:12;i:0;s:2:"01";i:0;s:2:"02";i:0;s:2:"03";i:0;s:2:"04";i:0;s:2:"05";i:0;s:2:"06";i:0;s:2:"07";i:0;s:2:"08";i:130;s:2:"09";i:22;s:1:"T";i:161;}s:8:"Category";s:21:"Tools:System Plugins:";s:2:"ID";i:2771;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:13:"DockerHubName";s:10:"firesquire";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:18:"Junker der Provinz";s:8:"SortName";s:10:"FireSquire";s:6:"random";i:1883061931;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:4:"Path";s:205:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/JunkerderProvinz-FireSquire-//raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire/main/plugin/firesquire.plg.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2772;a:40:{s:4:"Name";s:6:"Garage";s:10:"Repository";s:30:"junkerderprovinz/garage:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:48:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/garage";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:6:"ReadMe";s:62:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/garage/blob/main/README.md";s:7:"Support";s:76:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/198811-support-junkerderprovinz-unraid-apps/";s:7:"Project";s:43:"https://git.deuxfleurs.fr/Deuxfleurs/garage";s:8:"Overview";s:1528:"Garage is a lightweight, S3-API-compatible object store built for small, self-hosted deployments - Rust, Apache-2.0, actively developed. This template bundles the official Garage server AND its web admin panel (garage-webui, also official/unmodified) into ONE container, running single-node mode.

Why one container: Garage's own Community Applications listings are split across three separate templates from another maintainer (the core server in two network variants, plus the admin panel as its own separate install). This template combines the core server and the admin panel - both official, unmodified upstream binaries - into one install.

Zero manual CLI setup:
• Single-node cluster layout is created automatically at startup (Garage's own --single-node flag, v2.3.0+) - the manual "garage layout assign / apply" steps a bare install normally needs are not required here.
• An S3 access key, secret key and bucket are pre-seeded from the fields below on first boot - no second CLI step to create them either.
• garage-webui runs alongside it and talks to Garage's admin API over localhost - buckets, keys and cluster health are all browsable in the browser, no terminal needed.

First start: watch the container log for "GARAGE IS READY" - first boot generates the config, this takes a few seconds.

IMPORTANT: map BOTH the Data and Config volumes. Config holds the generated garage.toml (RPC secret, admin token) - losing it regenerates those secrets on next boot and breaks the admin panel's session.";s:5:"WebUI";s:23:"http://[IP]:[PORT:3909]";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:85:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/garage/garage.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:87:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/garage/icon.png?v=3";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:24:"--restart=unless-stopped";s:8:"Requires";s:123:"Set an Access Key and Secret Key below before storing anything real. See the README for client setup (rclone, restic, ...).";s:6:"Config";a:11:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"S3 API Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3900";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3900";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:127:"The S3-compatible endpoint every client (rclone, restic, aws-cli, ...) connects to. This is the port that matters functionally.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3900";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"Admin Panel Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3909";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3909";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:98:"Browser-based admin panel (garage-webui): buckets, keys, cluster health. This is what WebUI opens.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3909";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"S3 Website Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3902";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3902";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:79:"Optional. Static-site hosting straight from a bucket (Garage's s3_web feature).";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3902";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"Data";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Default";s:29:"/mnt/user/appdata/garage/data";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:80:"Object data and metadata. This MUST be mapped or everything is lost on recreate.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:29:"/mnt/user/appdata/garage/data";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:6:"Config";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:31:"/mnt/user/appdata/garage/config";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:166:"Generated garage.toml (RPC secret, admin token). This MUST be mapped - losing it regenerates your RPC/admin secrets on next boot and breaks the admin panel's session.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:31:"/mnt/user/appdata/garage/config";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Access Key";s:6:"Target";s:10:"ACCESS_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:156:"S3 access key, pre-seeded on first boot. Required to write anything real - leaving this and Secret Key empty means no bucket/key gets created automatically.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Secret Key";s:6:"Target";s:10:"SECRET_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:123:"S3 secret key, paired with Access Key above. Use a real generated secret (e.g. openssl rand -hex 24), not a short password.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:6:"Bucket";s:6:"Target";s:6:"BUCKET";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:154:"Optional. Name of a bucket to create automatically on first boot (e.g. backups). Leave empty to create buckets later via the admin panel or any S3 client.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s: