"FirstSeen";i:1602676263;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1790929862;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1791198847;s:11:"topTrending";d:0.07800000000000029;s:13:"topPerforming";d:4.33;s:8:"Category";s:36:"Productivity: Tools:Utilities Other:";s:2:"ID";i:2779;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:5:"n8nio";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:3:"n8n";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:5:"n8nio";s:8:"SortName";s:3:"n8n";s:6:"random";i:149915035;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:111:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/n8nio-n8n-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"trendDelta";d:0.078;s:12:"trendAverage";d:4.8671;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2780;a:43:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"OpenCloud";s:10:"Repository";s:34:"junkerderprovinz/opencloud:rolling";s:8:"Registry";s:51:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/opencloud";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:6:"ReadMe";s:65:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/opencloud/blob/main/README.md";s:7:"Support";s:74:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/200022-support-junkerderprovinz-opencloud/";s:7:"Project";s:20:"https://opencloud.eu";s:8:"Overview";s:6492:"OpenCloud - a modern, self-hosted file sync and share platform (a lightweight OpenCloud/ownCloud-family server) - packaged as a genuine one-click Unraid app. The official OpenCloud image has no PUID/PGID support and needs a manual init step, so a fresh install normally fails with "permission denied". This wrapper fixes that: it runs the one-time init for you, heals the appdata permissions and runs as your chosen PUID/PGID (99/100 = nobody/users by default).

Important, before the first start: keep every OpenCloud folder off /mnt/user.
OpenCloud has no database. Its metadata and message bus are files that it writes all the time, and a large sync through Unraid's share layer (any /mnt/user/... path) can hang that layer. Every container that uses it hangs along, and only a reboot helps.
• Config: the SSD pool itself, for example /mnt/cache/appdata/opencloud/config
• Data: the SSD pool itself, for example /mnt/cache/appdata/opencloud/data
• Files (optional, if your files don't fit on the pool): one array disk, for example /mnt/disk1/opencloud/files, with the share behind it set to that one disk
Use the pool and disk names from your Main tab. At startup the container log warns about any path that still goes through the share layer.

What's pre-configured:
• One-click: auto-runs 'opencloud init' on first boot, then the server - nothing to do on the console.
• Permission heal: root-created appdata folders are handed to your PUID/PGID automatically.
• Serves HTTPS itself on port 9200 (self-signed by default) - no reverse proxy required.
• Two channels via the Repository tag: :rolling (default, newest features) and :latest (stable/production). Both track OpenCloud's own upstream release automatically, no manual version bump needed.

Getting started:
• Set an Admin Password below - it is applied on the first start. Log in as user "admin".
• FIRST START takes a moment: the container generates its config and a self-signed certificate. Wait for the "OPENCLOUD IS READY" banner in the container log, then open the WebUI.
• Open the WebUI at https://[IP]:9200 and accept the self-signed certificate once.

Notes:
• Public URL: Unraid does NOT auto-fill the "Public URL" field. Set it to https://YOUR-SERVER-IP:9200 (your Unraid LAN IP), or your external https URL behind a reverse proxy. Leaving the placeholder lets the container guess its own IP so it still starts, but client logins only work reliably once it is correct.
• Storage driver: keep it on 'posix' (the default). A blank driver or the unsupported 'local' driver makes files show up but stay greyed out (not openable/downloadable). The Data folder must sit on a filesystem with extended-attribute support - the Unraid array and cache/pool disks have it; if files stay greyed out on an exotic share, move Data to a normal appdata/cache-backed path.
• Use a FRESH, empty Data folder. Do NOT point Data at an old OpenCloud/oCIS data directory: the on-disk layout differs between versions and the storage backends (local vs S3) are not interchangeable, so the server crash-loops (symptom in the log: search 'cannot open index, metadata missing'). There is no in-place migration between storage backends - to keep old files, start fresh and re-upload them.
• Why rolling is the default: OpenCloud's production line is currently 7.2.x, where a single transient NATS timeout ends the whole server process. Slow storage is exactly what produces those timeouts, so the risk is real on an array-backed setup. Rolling carries the fix from 7.5.0 onwards. Production releases about every six months, so the stable line will not pick it up for a while.
• Production channel: change the Repository tag from ':rolling' to ':latest' if you prefer OpenCloud's fully QA'd line over the newest features. Keep your appdata backed up before switching either way.
• Reverse proxy: if you terminate TLS in front of OpenCloud, set "OpenCloud terminates TLS" to 'false' (advanced) and set the Public URL to your external https URL (e.g. https://cloud.example.com). Then "Allow self-signed TLS" can be 'false'. Your proxy must connect to the container over plain HTTP on port 9200 (not HTTPS) once "OpenCloud terminates TLS" is 'false'.
• PUID/PGID default to 99/100 (nobody/users on Unraid).

S3 object storage (optional, advanced):
• OpenCloud can store file BLOBS in any S3-compatible bucket. Two genuine, actively-maintained self-hosted options: SeaweedFS (https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/Unraid-apps/tree/main/seaweedfs, recommended for a single node) and Garage (https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/garage, built for multi-node clusters, runs single-node here too). AWS, Backblaze B2 and Wasabi also work. Set the "Storage driver" to 'decomposeds3' and fill the S3 endpoint / region / access key / secret key / bucket fields. Keep the driver on 'posix' (the default) for normal local storage - do NOT leave it blank.
• IMPORTANT: only the file blobs go to S3. The file-tree METADATA always stays on the local Data volume - so that volume is required even with S3 and MUST be backed up. Losing it orphans your S3 objects (they are opaque IDs with no folder structure).
• OpenCloud does NOT use a database (no MySQL/Postgres) - do not look for one. State lives in the local metadata tree plus an embedded message bus.

How to get the S3 bucket and keys:
• SeaweedFS (self-hosted): in that template, set an Access Key and Secret Key (see its README's Security note) and optionally a Pre-create Bucket name - those become your access key, secret key and bucket directly, no separate service-account step. Endpoint is its S3 port, http://SEAWEEDFS-IP:8333, region 'default'.
• Garage (self-hosted): in that template, set an Access Key and Secret Key (and optionally a Bucket) - pre-seeded on first boot, no separate CLI step. Endpoint is its S3 API port, http://GARAGE-IP:3900, region 'garage'.
• AWS S3 / Backblaze B2 / Wasabi: create a bucket in the provider console, then create an access key (AWS: IAM access key; B2/Wasabi: an application/API key). Use the provider's https endpoint and the bucket's region.
• If uploads fail with a checksum error on a non-AWS endpoint, add STORAGE_USERS_DECOMPOSEDS3_PUT_OBJECT_DISABLE_CONTENT_SHA256=true as an extra variable.

Source on GitHub: https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/opencloud
