aid's share layer unless appdata is an exclusive share, and a large sync can hang that layer until the server is rebooted. If your SSD is too small for all your files, set Files below to a single array disk and leave Data here.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:32:"/mnt/user/appdata/opencloud/data";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:5:"Files";s:6:"Target";s:6:"/files";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:661:"Optional: where your files are stored, for example a single array disk such as /mnt/disk1/opencloud/files. Give the disk itself, not a /mnt/user share: a large sync can hang the share layer and every container on it. Left empty, the files stay inside Data. Set it when Data is on a small SSD or cache pool: the files then go here, and Data keeps only the message bus, search index and accounts, the parts that write to disk all the time and stall on the array. Set it before the first start. Added to an install that already has files, it starts with an empty folder and the old files stay in Data until you move them over (guide, 'Files in a separate folder').";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Admin Password";s:6:"Target";s:18:"IDM_ADMIN_PASSWORD";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:155:"Password for the built-in 'admin' user. REQUIRED. Applied on the first start (during init). Choose a strong value - this is the login for your whole cloud.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Public URL";s:6:"Target";s:6:"OC_URL";s:7:"Default";s:27:"https://YOUR-SERVER-IP:9200";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:496:"REQUIRED. The URL clients use to reach OpenCloud, and the login (OIDC) issuer. Unraid does NOT auto-fill this: replace YOUR-SERVER-IP with your Unraid server's LAN IP, e.g. https://192.168.1.10:9200 (keep the https:// and the :9200 port). Behind a reverse proxy set your external https URL instead, e.g. https://cloud.example.com. Must be https. If you leave the placeholder, the container falls back to its own IP so it still boots, but client logins may not work until you set the real address.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:27:"https://YOUR-SERVER-IP:9200";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:21:"Allow self-signed TLS";s:6:"Target";s:11:"OC_INSECURE";s:7:"Default";s:10:"true|false";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:166:"'true' (default) accepts the container's self-signed certificate - correct for a direct Unraid install. Set 'false' when a reverse proxy provides a valid certificate.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"true";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"Log Level";s:6:"Target";s:12:"OC_LOG_LEVEL";s:7:"Default";s:21:"info|warn|error|debug";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:31:"Verbosity of the container log.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"info";}i:8;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"Create Demo Users";s:6:"Target";s:21:"IDM_CREATE_DEMO_USERS";s:7:"Default";s:10:"false|true";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:154:"'true' seeds a set of demo users (alan, mary, ...) with well-known passwords - handy for testing, unsafe for real use. Keep 'false' for a real deployment.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:9;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:24:"OpenCloud terminates TLS";s:6:"Target";s:9:"PROXY_TLS";s:7:"Default";s:10:"true|false";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:280:"'true' (default) = OpenCloud serves HTTPS itself on 9200 (self-signed) - correct for a direct Unraid install. Set 'false' when a reverse proxy terminates TLS in front of the container; then also set the Public URL to your external https URL and 'Allow self-signed TLS' to 'false'.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"true";}i:10;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:27:"App tokens (WebDAV clients)";s:6:"Target";s:21:"PROXY_ENABLE_APP_AUTH";s:7:"Default";s:10:"false|true";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:529:"'true' lets a WebDAV client sign in with a username and an APP TOKEN, created under Account -&gt; App tokens in the web interface. rclone, phone sync apps and anything else that cannot do the browser sign-in need this; without it every such request is refused with 401 before the token is even read, which looks exactly like a wrong token. It does NOT enable the account password for WebDAV - that is the separate PROXY_ENABLE_BASIC_AUTH, which stays off. A token can be revoked on its own, without changing the account password.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:11;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Branding admin app";s:6:"Target";s:12:"BRANDING_APP";s:7:"Default";s:10:"false|true";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:1057:"'false' (default) = no Branding app. 'true' adds one to the web interface, where accounts with the Admin role set the instance name, slogan, logo (plus one for dark mode), favicon, login background and whether the sign-in card is light, dark or follows the browser; other accounts do not see it, and the small service behind it (proxy route /brandingsvc/) checks every change with OpenCloud and refuses theirs. The name shows in the browser tab and on the login page, the slogan on the login page and on public link pages. Saved changes apply without a restart, on the login page too; only switching this setting needs one. Switching back to 'false' removes the app and keeps the saved branding, but on the login page only your logo and background remain. With your own /etc/opencloud/proxy.yaml the app turns on only if that file has the /brandingsvc/ route. With your own IDP_ASSET_PATH the login page keeps its own title, footer, favicon and card, and adding the first login background, or removing it, needs a restart. See the README 'Branding' section.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:12;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"User ID";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PUID";s:7:"Default";s:2:"99";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:112:"User-ID OpenCloud runs as. Default 99 (nobody on Unraid). The wrapper heals appdata ownership to match on start.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:2:"99";}i:13;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:8:"Group ID";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PGID";s:7:"Default";s:3:"100";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:40:"Group-ID. Default 100 (users on Unraid).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:3:"100";}i:14;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Storage driver";s:6:"Target";s:20:"STORAGE_USERS_DRIVER";s:7:"Default";s:18:"posix|decomposeds3";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:525:"How OpenCloud stores your files. Keep 'posix' (the default) for a normal local install - files live on the Data volume. Choose 'decomposeds3' ONLY to keep file blobs in an S3 bucket (then fill the five S3 fields below); even then the metadata tree stays on the local Data volume and must be backed up. Do NOT leave this blank and never type 'local' - both break file access (files appear but are greyed out and cannot be opened or downloaded). The driver is fixed at first start; to change it, start with a fresh Data folder.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"posix";}i:15;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"S3 endpoint";s:6:"Target";s:35:"STORAGE_USERS_DECOMPOSEDS3_ENDPOINT";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:278:"S3 API endpoint URL. Self-hosted SeaweedFS: the internal http URL on its S3 port 8333, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:8333. Self-hosted Garage: its S3 API port 3900, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:3900. AWS/B2/Wasabi: their https endpoint. Only used when the driver above is 'decomposeds3'.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:16;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"S3 region";s:6:"Target";s:33:"STORAGE_USERS_DECOMPOSEDS3_REGION";s:7:"Default";s:166:"default|garage|us-east-1|us-east-2|us-west-1|us-west-2|eu-central-1|eu-west-1|eu-west-2|eu-north-1|ap-southeast-1|ap-southeast-2|ap-northeast-1|ca-central-1|sa-east-1";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:216:"Bucket region. Use 'default' for self-hosted SeaweedFS, 'garage' for self-hosted Garage (its default region), or your provider's region (e.g. eu-central-1) for AWS/B2/Wasabi. Only used with the 'decomposeds3' driver.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}i:17;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"S3 access key";s:6:"Target";s:37:"STORAGE_USERS_DECOMPOSEDS3_ACCESS_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:180:"Access key ID for the bucket. Self-hosted SeaweedFS/Garage: the Access Key you set on that container's template directly. AWS: an IAM access key; B2/Wasabi: an application/API key.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:18;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"S3 secret key";s:6:"Target";s:37:"STORAGE_USERS_DECOMPOSEDS3_SECRET_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:149:"The secret that pairs with the access key. Self-hosted SeaweedFS/Garage: the Secret Key you set on that container's template directly. Stored masked.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:19;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"S3 bucket";s:6:"Target";s:33:"STORAGE_USERS_DECOMPOSEDS3_BUCKET";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:303:"Name of the S3 bucket to store blobs in. Create it FIRST - the container does not. SeaweedFS: use its own 'Pre-create Bucket' template field. Garage: use its own 'Bucket' template field. Either way, or with any S3 tool. AWS/B2/Wasabi: create a bucket in the provider console. Enter that exact name here.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:20;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"Web office suite";s:6:"Target";s:6:"OFFICE";s:7:"Default";s:36:"off|euro-office|collabora|onlyoffice";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:205:"Turns on editing documents in the browser. The editor runs as its own container, for example the Euro Office template. Choices: off, euro-office, collabora, onlyoffice. See the README section 'Web office'.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:3:"off";}i:21;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:26:"Office document server URL";s:6:"Target";s:17:"OFFICE_SERVER_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:284:"Address of the Euro Office container on your network, the one its WebUI button opens, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:9900. OpenCloud passes the editor through its own address, so plain http works and there is no certificate to accept. For Collabora or OnlyOffice, enter their https address.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:22;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Office WOPI secret";s:6:"Target";s:18:"OFFICE_WOPI_SECRET";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:176:"The same text as the JWT secret in the Euro Office container. If they differ, documents fail with 'document security token is not correctly formed'. Collabora does not need it.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:23;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:23:"Full-text search (Tika)";s:6:"Target";s:15:"FULLTEXT_SEARCH";s:7:"Default";s:10:"false|true";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:590:"Search inside file CONTENTS (PDF, Word, Excel, ...), not just file names. 'false' (default) = OpenCloud's normal name/metadata search. 'true' = content search via Apache Tika. Tika ALWAYS runs as a SEPARATE container that you install and point to below: install a Tika template from Community Applications (search 'Tika'), image apache/tika, port 9998 (a '-full' tag also OCRs scanned images). NOTE: only files uploaded or changed AFTER you enable this are content-indexed; existing files are not re-indexed automatically (re-upload or edit them). See the README 'Full-text search' section.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"false";}i:24;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Tika server URL";s:6:"Target";s:8:"TIKA_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:135:"Network-reachable URL of your Apache Tika container, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:9998. Only used when 'Full-text search (Tika)' is 'true'.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:43:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/opencloud/opencloud.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:5:"Cloud";i:1;s:13:"Network-Other";}s:9:"downloads";i:25558;s:13:"downloadtrend";a:2:{i:0;i:11218;i:1;i:25558;}s:8:"trending";d:56.108;s:6:"trends";a:1:{i:0;d:56.108;}s:10:"trendsDate";a:1:{i:0;i:1790454204;}s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1785248890;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1790360239;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1790454204;s:8:"Category";s:20:"Cloud: Network:Other";s:2:"ID";i:2780;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:9:"opencloud";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:8:"SortName";s:9:"OpenCloud";s:6:"random";i:536244651;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:129:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/junkerderprovinz-OpenCloud-rolling.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2781;a:37:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"OpenHands";s:10:"Repository";s:44:"docker.openhands.dev/openhands/openhands:1.7";s:8:"Registry";s:41:"https://github.com/All-Hands-AI/OpenHands";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:76:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/198811-support-junkerderprovinz-unraid-apps/";s:7:"Project";s:38:"https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands";s:6:"ReadMe";s:77:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/blob/main/openhands/README.md";s:8:"Overview";s:340:"OpenHands is an open-source AI-driven development agent. It writes, reviews, and tests code. Important: Requires access to the Docker socket to spin up isolated execution environments. Pre-configured for local Ollama use.Detailed README (full setup & options): https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/Unraid-apps/blob/main/openhands/README.md";s:5:"WebUI";s:24:"http://[IP]:[PORT:3000]/";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:91:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/openhands/openhands.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:86:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/openhands/icon.png";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:44:"--add-host host.docker.internal:host-gateway";s:8:"Requires";s:190:"A reachable Ollama instance (or any OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint) configured via the LLM_* variables. Defaults assume Ollama running on the Unraid host, reachable via host.docker.internal.";s:6:"Config";a:9:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:5:"WebUI";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3000";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3000";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:23:"OpenHands web interface";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3000";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"Config Directory";s:6:"Target";s:11:"/.openhands";s:7:"Default";s:27:"/mnt/user/appdata/openhands";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:44:"Persistent OpenHands configuration and state";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:27:"/mnt/user/appdata/openhands";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:19:"Workspace Directory";s:6:"Target";s:10:"/workspace";s:7:"Default";s:22:"/mnt/user/ai-workspace";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:38:"Working directory exposed to the agent";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:22:"/mnt/user/ai-workspace";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Docker Socket";s:6:"Target";s:20:"/var/run/docker.sock";s:7:"Default";s:20:"/var/run/docker.sock";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:59:"Required so OpenHands can spawn isolated sandbox containers";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:20:"/var/run/docker.sock";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Sandbox Volumes";s:6:"Target";s:15:"SANDBOX_VOLUMES";s:7:"Default";s:36:"/mnt/user/ai-workspace:/workspace:rw";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:125:"Host:container mount(s) injected into every sandbox container the agent spins up. Must match the Workspace Directory mapping.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:36:"/mnt/user/ai-workspace:/workspace:rw";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"LLM API Key";s:6:"Target";s:11:"LLM_API_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:6:"ollama";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:82:"API key for the LLM backend. For Ollama any non-empty value works (e.g. 'ollama').";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:6:"ollama";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"LLM Model";s:6:"Target";s:9:"LLM_MODEL";s:7:"Default";s:24:"ollama/qwen2.5-coder:14b";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:74:"LiteLLM-style model string. Default targets Ollama with qwen2.5-coder:14b.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:24:"ollama/qwen2.5-coder:14b";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:12:"LLM Base URL";s:6:"Target";s:12:"LLM_BASE_URL";s:7:"Default";s:33:"http://host.docker.internal:11434";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:231:"Base URL of the LLM endpoint. Default reaches Ollama on the Unraid host via host.docker.internal. For a remote/LAN Ollama use http://host:11434. Leave blank for hosted providers (OpenAI/Anthropic/etc.) that use their own endpoints.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:33:"http://host.docker.internal:11434";}i:8;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:19:"LLM Embedding Model";s:6:"Target";s:19:"LLM_EMBEDDING_MODEL";s:7:"Default";s:5:"local";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:82:"Embedding model used for memory/context. 'local' uses the bundled embedding model.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:5:"local";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:43:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/openhands/openhands.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:3:{i:0;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";i:1;s:12:"Productivity";i:2;s:2:"AI";}s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1781028981;s:8:"Category";s:33:"Tools:Utilities Productivity: AI:";s:2:"ID";i:2781;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:9:"openhands";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:9:"openhands";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:9:"openhands";s:8:"SortName";s:9:"OpenHands";s:6:"random";i:366540206;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:118:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/openhands-OpenHands-1.7.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2782;a:38:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"ParleyPort";s:10:"Repository";s:34:"junkerderprovinz/parleyport:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:52:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/parleyport";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:53:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/parleyport/issues";s:7:"Project";s:46:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/parleyport";s:6:"ReadMe";s:66:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/parleyport/blob/main/README.md";s:8:"Overview";s:1188:"Your own relay for KnightLoader and BombVault.

Both apps pair their instances with twelve words. Instances on the same network talk directly, but two instances on different networks, behind NAT or a firewall that lets nothing in, need a third point both can dial out to. ParleyPort is that point. It groups the connections that share a relay key and passes their messages on, sealed end to end with a key that never leaves your instances, so it cannot read what it carries.

You only need it if you want your own relay. The project relay at relay.halleluja.design is the default in both apps, and an instance that is already reachable from outside can serve as the relay itself. ParleyPort is for running your own without putting a download manager or a backup tool on the open internet: a few megabytes, no accounts, no database, nothing stored.

Plain mode listens on port 8760 for a reverse proxy in front, which must allow WebSocket upgrades on /relay/connect. Set a domain and map port 443 instead, and it fetches and renews its own Let's Encrypt certificate, with port 80 left closed. Then enter the address under Settings, Pairing, Relay, Own relay on every instance of the group.";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:93:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/parleyport/parleyport.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:91:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/unraid-apps/main/parleyport/icon.png?v=2";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:24:"--restart unless-stopped";s:6:"Config";a:6:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Relay Port (plain)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"8760";s:7:"Default";s:4:"8760";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:162:"Plain HTTP for a reverse proxy in front. Point the proxy at this port and allow WebSocket upgrades on /relay/connect. /health answers with the status and version.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"8760";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"Relay Port (TLS)";s:6:"Target";s:3:"443";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:180:"Only with a domain set below. Enter the host port your router forwards 443 to. Leave it empty in plain mode, and keep it off 443 if the Unraid web interface already uses that port.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:6:"Domain";s:6:"Target";s:17:"PARLEYPORT_DOMAIN";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:319:"Your relay's domain, for example relay.example.org. With it set, ParleyPort serves TLS on 443 inside the container and gets its own Let's Encrypt certificate through port 443 alone. Several names can be given, separated by commas, to keep an old name working during a move. Leave it empty for plain mode behind a proxy.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Public URL";s:6:"Target";s:21:"PARLEYPORT_PUBLIC_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:301:"The address your instances dial, for example https://relay.example.org, or several separated by commas. A KnightLoader instance proves who it is only to the relay it names when it connects, so set this in plain mode behind a proxy. In domain mode the names above already count and this can stay empty.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Trusted proxies";s:6:"Target";s:26:"PARLEYPORT_TRUSTED_PROXIES";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:365:"For a reverse proxy in another container or on another machine: its address, for example 172.18.0.5, or a range no wider than a /28 (IPv4) or /124 (IPv6). Separate several with commas. Failed handshakes then count against the caller and not against the proxy everyone comes through. Left empty, only a proxy on the same host is trusted; none trusts no proxy at all.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"Data";s:6:"Target";s:19:"/var/lib/parleyport";s:7:"Default";s:28:"/mnt/user/appdata/parleyport";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:120:"Holds the certificates in domain mode, so a restart does not ask Let's Encrypt for a new one. Stays empty in plain mode.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:28:"/mnt/user/appdata/parleyport";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:45:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/parleyport/parleyport.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:13:"Network-Other";i:1;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";}s:9:"downloads";i:134;s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1790969388;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1790897348;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1790969388;s:8:"Category";s:29:"Network:Other Tools:Utilities";s:2:"ID";i:2782;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:10:"parleyport";s:8:"RepoName";s:29:"junkerderprovinz's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:16:"junkerderprovinz";s:8:"SortName";s:10:"ParleyPort";s:6:"random";i:187498110;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:129:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/junkerderprovinzsRepository/junkerderprovinz-ParleyPort-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:2783;a:39:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"PrusaSlicer";s:10:"Repository";s:35:"junkerderprovinz/prusaslicer:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:53:"https://hub.docker.com/r/junkerderprovinz/prusaslicer";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:4:"bash";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:6:"ReadMe";s:67:"https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/prusaslicer/blob/main/README.md";s:7:"Support";s:76:"https://forums.unraid.net/topic/198811-support-junkerderprovinz-unraid-apps/";s:7:"Project";s:36:"https://www.prusa3d.com/prusaslicer/";s:8:"Overview";s:1742:"PrusaSlicer, the FDM/SLA 3D-printing slicer from Prusa Research, on a Selkies web desktop. Slice in your browser: no VNC client, no local install. LinuxServer ship OrcaSlicer and Cura on Selkies but no PrusaSlicer; the only other browser images are years-old, abandoned noVNC builds. This is a maintained, modern Selkies (WebRTC) build for amd64 and arm64.

What you get:
• The full PrusaSlicer desktop app, streamed as H.264 video (Selkies) so the 3D plate stays smooth to rotate, zoom and drag, the part of slicing where the old noVNC containers feel laggy.
• Installed from Debian trixie's prusa-slicer package (PrusaSlicer no longer ships a Linux AppImage), so it tracks Debian security updates.
• PrusaSlicer's own Dark mode (Preferences → General); dark GTK chrome by default.
• Printer / filament / print profiles persist under /config.

Notes:
• WebUI opens via HTTPS on port 3001 (self-signed cert, accept it once). The Selkies web client requires a secure connection, so open the WebUI directly ONLY over HTTPS (3001). Port 3000 (HTTP) is for a reverse proxy that terminates TLS in front of the container; opening it directly shows a "requires a secure connection (HTTPS)" error.
• Mount your models / G-code folder to /storage (default /mnt/user) so imports and slices land on your array.
• PUID/PGID default to 99/100 (nobody/users on Unraid).
• Auth: leave "WebUI Username" and "Password" EMPTY to run without login. Set both to enable HTTP-basic auth on the WebUI. Recommended whenever the container is reachable beyond your LAN.
• GPU: a passed-through GPU is used when present; otherwise it falls back to software rendering.

