.io/theduffman85/crowdsec-web-ui:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:80:"https://github.com/users/TheDuffman85/packages/container/package/crowdsec-web-ui";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:4:"bash";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:54:"https://github.com/TheDuffman85/crowdsec-web-ui/issues";s:7:"Project";s:47:"https://github.com/TheDuffman85/crowdsec-web-ui";s:8:"Overview";s:1179:"CrowdSec Web UI, a self-hosted dashboard for CrowdSec: investigate alerts, manage decisions, monitor runtime metrics, and send notifications from one responsive UI.

The UI connects to the CrowdSec Local API (LAPI) as a watcher machine. Register it on your CrowdSec container before starting this one, using a strong password (e.g. from openssl rand -hex 32):
docker exec crowdsec cscli machines add crowdsec-web-ui --password 'your-password' -f /dev/null
Keep -f /dev/null so the CrowdSec container's own credentials file is not overwritten.

Set the LAPI URL to an address reachable from this container: http://crowdsec:8080 when both containers share a custom Docker network, or http://[Unraid-IP]:8080 otherwise. On first visit, create the initial administrator account.

Alert deletion additionally requires this container's IP or Docker network CIDR to be listed under api.server.trusted_ips in CrowdSec's config.yaml; without it, deletes fail with 403 Forbidden.

