https://forums.unraid.net/topic/123985-timemachine-application-support-thread/";s:7:"Project";s:46:"https://github.com/mbentley/docker-timemachine";s:8:"Overview";s:185:"This application provides a Samba based Time Machine network share. It can be configured to support multiple or single users and restrict the maximum size used for Time Machine backups.";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:92:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/moritzfl/unraid-templates/main/timemachine/timemachine.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:80:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/de/f/f4/Time_Machine_%28Apple%29_Logo.png";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:22:"--hostname timemachine";s:8:"Requires";s:902:"You will need to set the filesystem permissions on disk. By default, the timemachine user is 1000:1000.<br><br>Granting Permissions to write to the directory can be achieved by executing the following command on the host (Unraid): sudo chown -R 1000:1000 /mnt/user/timemachine/<br><br>The backing data store for your persistent time machine data must support extended file attributes (xattr). Usually for shares in the Unraid Array this is not a problem. Remote file systems, such as NFS, will very likely not support xattrs. This image will check and try to set xattrs to a test file in /opt/${TM_USERNAME} to warn the user if they are not supported but this will not prevent the image from running.<br><br>Also note that if you change the User Name (TM_USERNAME) value that it will change the data path for backup data inside of the container from /opt/timemachine to /opt/value-of-TM_USERNAME.";s:6:"Config";a:22:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:24:"Time Machine Backup Data";s:6:"Target";s:16:"/opt/timemachine";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:178:"Directory to store the time machine backups. If you change the User Name (TM_USERNAME) value that it will change the data path from /opt/timemachine to /opt/value-of-TM_USERNAME.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:22:"/mnt/user/timemachine/";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Size Limit";s:6:"Target";s:17:"VOLUME_SIZE_LIMIT";s:7:"Default";s:3:"1 T";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:636:"Limits the reported disksize, thus preventing Time Machine from using the whole real disk space for backup. The option takes a number plus an optional unit (e.g. 1 T for 1 Terrabyte).

IMPORTANT: This is an approximated calculation that only takes into account the contents of Time Machine sparsebundle images. Therefore you MUST NOT use this volume to store other content when using this option, because it would NOT be accounted.
