4:"Private Certificate Authority for Unraid, providing trusted HTTPS
certificates for services on your LAN and an internal ACME server for
automated certificate issuance - built on Smallstep's open-source step-ca.

Rather than self-signing each internal service's certificate separately
(and having to trust every one of them individually), step-ca lets you run
your own CA: trust its single root certificate once, and every certificate
it issues - for any internal hostname - is trusted automatically. Pair it
with an ACME-capable reverse proxy to issue and renew those certificates
without any manual steps.

step-ca can also issue SSH certificates and be used as a general-purpose
X.509 CA; the ACME/TLS use case above is just the most common reason to
run it on Unraid.

On first start (when the appdata directory is empty), the container
initializes a brand-new root and intermediate CA using the "CA Name" and
"DNS Names" values below. Keep the appdata volume safe afterwards: it
holds your root CA's private key, and must survive container recreation
and image upgrades.";s:7:"Support";s:55:"https://github.com/simonlehmann/unraid-templates/issues";s:7:"Project";s:41:"https://github.com/smallstep/certificates";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:88:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/simonlehmann/unraid-templates/main/step-ca/step-ca.xml";s:6:"ReadMe";s:86:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/simonlehmann/unraid-templates/main/step-ca/README.md";s:16:"ExtraSearchTerms";s:56:"acme pki tls ssh certificate authority ca smallstep step";s:8:"Requires";s:198:"Set &quot;CA Name&quot; and &quot;DNS Names&quot; before the first start - they are only applied while the appdata path is empty. Internet access is only needed to pull the image, not to run the CA.";s:4:"Date";s:10:"2026-09-06";s:7:"License";s:10:"Apache-2.0";s:6:"Config";a:10:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"CA Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"9000";s:7:"Default";s:4:"9000";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:51:"Port the CA listens on for HTTPS and ACME requests.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"Appdata";s:6:"Target";s:10:"/home/step";s:7:"Default";s:25:"/mnt/user/appdata/step-ca";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:145:"Persistent CA state: root/intermediate keys, database and config. Must be empty the first time you start the container so step-ca can initialize.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"CA Name";s:6:"Target";s:23:"DOCKER_STEPCA_INIT_NAME";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:89:"Friendly name for your CA, e.g. 'Home Lab CA'. Only read during first-run initialization.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"DNS Names";s:6:"Target";s:28:"DOCKER_STEPCA