o";s:16:"svk's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:3:{i:0;s:14:"HomeAutomation";i:1;s:12:"Productivity";i:2;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";}s:16:"ChangeLogPresent";s:1:"1";s:5:"stars";i:4;s:9:"downloads";i:785402;s:13:"downloadtrend";a:7:{i:0;i:616090;i:1;i:632956;i:2;i:650119;i:3;i:664414;i:4;i:685659;i:5;i:728825;i:6;i:785402;}s:8:"trending";d:7.204;s:6:"trends";a:7:{i:0;d:2.384;i:1;d:2.665;i:2;d:2.64;i:3;d:2.152;i:4;d:3.098;i:5;d:5.923;i:6;d:7.204;}s:10:"trendsDate";a:7:{i:0;i:1775617841;i:1;i:1778217195;i:2;i:1780819383;i:3;i:1783411433;i:4;i:1786088880;i:5;i:1788682215;i:6;i:1791274300;}s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1700456760;s:10:"LastUpdate";i:1779010577;s:14:"LastUpdateScan";i:1791274300;s:11:"topTrending";d:4.82;s:13:"topPerforming";d:7.204;s:8:"Category";s:45:"HomeAutomation: Productivity: Tools:Utilities";s:2:"ID";i:4572;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:11:"skrashevich";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:11:"double-take";s:8:"RepoName";s:16:"svk's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:11:"skrashevich";s:8:"SortName";s:11:"double take";s:6:"random";i:283617736;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:115:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/svksRepository/skrashevich-doubletake-v1.13.11.8.xml";s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"trendDelta";d:4.82;s:12:"trendAverage";d:3.7237;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:4573;a:40:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"dupearr";s:10:"Repository";s:30:"ghcr.io/sl0wz3r/dupearr:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:57:"https://github.com/sl0wz3r/dupearr/pkgs/container/dupearr";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:41:"https://github.com/sl0wz3r/dupearr/issues";s:7:"Project";s:34:"https://github.com/sl0wz3r/dupearr";s:6:"ReadMe";s:61:"https://github.com/sl0wz3r/dupearr/blob/main/unraid/README.md";s:8:"Overview";s:2181:"Dupearr finds duplicate movies and TV episodes in your Plex libraries, keeps the best copy according to your own rules and removes the others. It adds what Radarr and Sonarr know about every copy (quality, source, custom-format score, release group, which instance tracks it), ranks the copies with an ordered decision profile (for example: highest resolution, then HDR, then remux) and explains every decision before anything happens.

**This app deletes media files.** It starts in its safest state: dry run is ON, so approved removals are only simulated and logged, and manual approval is ON, so nothing is queued until you approve a duplicate group yourself. Files are only removed after you turn dry run off (Settings - Media Management) AND approve a group. More guards: files younger than 7 days are never removed, each run stops at 25 files or 500 GB, the kept copy is re-verified right before every removal, a group always keeps at least one copy, suspicious matches are held for review and never auto-approved, and every action is logged and marked permanent or restorable. Turn on the recycle bin in Radarr/Sonarr and in Dupearr before you switch dry run off, and keep backups of anything you cannot replace.

What sets it apart: removals go through the Radarr or Sonarr instance that owns the file (so it is not downloaded again), through Plex, or into Dupearr's own recycle bin with one-click restore. Hardlinked copies that are still seeding are recognised and count as 0 bytes reclaimable, a 4K and a 1080p copy managed by separate Radarr/Sonarr instances are kept on purpose, Plex optimized versions are never touched, editions (Director's Cut, Extended), 3D and different-language copies are kept apart, and full Blu-ray/DVD disc backups count as one copy and are kept by default.

Arr-style web UI and API on port 3873: login, API key, scheduled scans, backups, notifications and webhooks. The first login asks for a one-time setup code printed in the container log. Map /data to the same host folder your Plex and Radarr/Sonarr containers use (TRaSH layout: /mnt/user/data) for filesystem removals, the recycle bin and hardlink detection. Beta: pre-1.0 software.";s:5:"WebUI";s:24:"http://[IP]:[PORT:3873]/";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:73:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sl0wz3r/dupearr/main/unraid/dupearr.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:70:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sl0wz3r/dupearr/main/unraid/icon.png";s:11:"ExtraParams";s:140:"--security-opt=no-new-privileges:true --cap-drop=ALL --cap-add=CHOWN --cap-add=DAC_OVERRIDE --cap-add=KILL --cap-add=SETGID --cap-add=SETUID";s:8:"Requires";s:587:"Plex Media Server (any install). Radarr (v5/v6) and Sonarr (v4) are optional but strongly recommended: removing through them keeps them in sync and prevents re-downloads. For the filesystem deletion method, the recycle bin and hardlink detection, map /data to the SAME host folder your Plex and Radarr/Sonarr containers use (TRaSH layout: /mnt/user/data) and add a Plex path mapping in Dupearr (Settings - Media Management; with identical paths map the folder to itself, e.g. /data/media to /data/media). Keep the Extra Parameters: they drop every capability the container does not need.";s:16:"ExtraSearchTerms";s:99:"duplicate duplicates dupe dupes dedupe deduplicate plex radarr sonarr arr cleanup versions 4k remux";s:7:"License";s:16:"GPL-3.0-or-later";s:4:"Beta";s:4:"true";s:6:"MinVer";s:4:"6.12";s:4:"Date";s:10:"2026-09-27";s:6:"Config";a:8:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"WebUI Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"3873";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3873";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:79:"Port of the Dupearr web UI and API. Change only the host port if 3873 is taken.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3873";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"Appdata";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:25:"/mnt/user/appdata/dupearr";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:155:"config.xml, the SQLite database, logs and backups (it also holds your Plex token and arr API keys). Keep it on a pool/cache disk, never on a network share.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:25:"/mnt/user/appdata/dupearr";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:5:"Media";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Default";s:14:"/mnt/user/data";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:486:"Mount the SAME host path your Plex/Radarr/Sonarr containers use (TRaSH layout: /mnt/user/data to /data) so file paths match, then add a Plex path mapping in Dupearr (identical paths: map the folder to itself). Needed only for the filesystem deletion method, the recycle bin and hardlink detection; Radarr/Sonarr and Plex removals work without it. Unraid creates this folder if it does not exist, so change it to your media share (e.g. /mnt/user/media) or clear it if you do not need it.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:14:"/mnt/user/data";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"PUID";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PUID";s:7:"Default";s:2:"99";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:100:"User id Dupearr runs as. It must be allowed to delete your media files (Unraid default 99 = nobody).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:2:"99";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"PGID";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PGID";s:7:"Default";s:3:"100";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:54:"Group id Dupearr runs as (Unraid default 100 = users).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:3:"100";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:5:"UMASK";s:6:"Target";s:5:"UMASK";s:7:"Default";s:3:"022";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:207:"umask for files Dupearr creates in shared folders (recycle bin, restored files). 022 is the Unraid default; 002 keeps them group-writable. Dupearr's own config, database, backups and logs are always private.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:3:"022";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Trusted proxies";s:6:"Target";s:29:"DUPEARR__AUTH__TRUSTEDPROXIES";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:453:"Only behind a reverse proxy (SWAG, Nginx Proxy Manager, ...): its IP address as Dupearr sees it (the proxy container's IP on a custom Docker network, not the gateway), comma-separated IPs or CIDR ranges. Dupearr then believes the client address the proxy reports and, with External authentication, trusts only requests the proxy relays. A value here overrides Trusted Proxies in Settings - General (read-only there); leave empty to set it there instead.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Allowed hosts";s:6:"Target";s:27:"DUPEARR__AUTH__ALLOWEDHOSTS";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:259:"Optional, with a reverse proxy: the host names Dupearr is reached by (e.g. dupearr.example.com; *.example.com for sub-domains), comma-separated. A value here overrides Allowed Hosts in Settings - General (read-only there); leave empty to set it there instead.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:38:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/unraid/dupearr.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:20:"slowz3r's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:14:"MediaApp-Video";i:1;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";}s:16:"ChangeLogPresent";s:1:"1";s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1790266484;s:8:"Category";s:30:"MediaApp:Video Tools:Utilities";s:2:"ID";i:4573;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:7:"sl0wz3r";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:7:"dupearr";s:8:"RepoName";s:20:"slowz3r's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:7:"sl0wz3r";s:8:"SortName";s:7:"dupearr";s:6:"random";i:1940045979;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:4:"Path";s:108:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/slowz3rsRepository/sl0wz3r-dupearr-latest.xml";s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:4574;a:50:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"Dispatcharr";s:10:"Repository";s:38:"ghcr.io/dispatcharr/dispatcharr:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:69:"https://github.com/Dispatcharr/Dispatcharr/pkgs/container/dispatcharr";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:29:"https://discord.gg/wfeqTRRJru";s:7:"Project";s:43:"https://github.com/Dispatcharr/Dispatcharr/";s:8:"Overview";s:1092:"Dispatcharr is a IPTV streaming playlist (m3u m3u8) editor and proxy.  IPTV proxy application that enables users to curate complex M3U playlists, manage them, and proxy them to various applications like Emby, Plex, Jellyfin, or any other compatible streaming platform. It offers the flexibility to import an unlimited number of M3U and EPG files, supporting a wide range of streaming content. Dispatcharr delivers fast and reliable streaming across multiple operating systems. Additional features, such as robust proxying and support for multiple clients on a single backend stream, make it a comprehensive solution for today’s streaming needs.  Proxy Streaming Engine - Stream more reliably, with fewer provider connections. Real-Time Stats Dashboard - Monitor stream health and client activity live. EPG Auto-Match - Automatically map guide data to your channels. Output Compatibility - Works with Plex, Jellyfin, Emby, ChannelsDVR and more (HDHomeRun, M3U, XMLTV).

