1:"1";s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1792354556;s:8:"Category";s:14:"MediaApp:Other";s:2:"ID";i:4583;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:13:"snapetech-org";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:8:"romarrng";s:8:"RepoName";s:21:"romarrng's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:13:"snapetech-org";s:8:"SortName";s:8:"ROMarrNG";s:6:"random";i:281354905;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:115:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/romarrngsRepository/snapetechorg-ROMarrNG-latest.xml";s:4:"Date";N;s:11:"BrandNewApp";b:1;s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:4584;a:38:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"torrentng-webui";s:10:"Repository";s:42:"ghcr.io/snapetech/torrentng/sidecar:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:73:"https://github.com/snapetech/TorrentNG/pkgs/container/torrentng%2Fsidecar";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:4:"Icon";s:71:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/main/icon.png";s:5:"WebUI";s:23:"http://[IP]:[PORT:8080]";s:8:"Overview";s:4375:"TorrentNG WebUI is a modern replacement web interface and API for an rTorrent, qBittorrent, Transmission, or Deluge install you already run -- on this box or elsewhere on your network. It does not run its own torrent engine; your existing client keeps owning transfer/session state. TorrentNG polls it, caches state for a fast virtualized 10k-100k-row table, pushes live updates over WebSocket, and exposes a qBittorrent-compatible API so Prowlarr/Sonarr/Radarr/autobrr/cross-seed keep working unchanged.

If you don't already run one of those four clients and just want one box that does everything, use the "TorrentNG" (native) template instead -- it runs TorrentNG's own first-party client with no external dependency.

SETUP: pick "Backend Type" below and fill in only that backend's section (all four are listed since Unraid templates can't hide fields conditionally -- the others are ignored):

- qBittorrent / Transmission / Deluge: point the matching URL variable at your existing container's WebUI/RPC address (e.g. http://192.168.1.10:8080 for qBittorrent). These already speak HTTP over the network, so this is normally a two-field job: URL + credentials.
- rTorrent: leave "Managed" at 0 (the default) to connect to an rTorrent you already run, and set "SCGI TCP Address" to host:port. This requires your existing rTorrent to have SCGI bound to a TCP address rather than a private Unix socket inside its own container -- check that client's own config/template. If your setup only exposes a Unix socket, mount the host directory holding that socket file into this container yourself (extra Path in this template's Unraid config) and point "SCGI Socket Path" at it instead. Set "Managed" to 1 only if you want this container to launch and own its own brand-new rTorrent (rarely what you want alongside an existing install).

IMPORTANT -- rTorrent identity: on every start, TorrentNG pushes its own User-Agent and peer_id to whatever rTorrent it's connected to (this is how the compatible API keeps *arr tools working). Pointing this at an rTorrent that already has an established tracker history under a different identity will silently rewrite that identity going forward. This is expected and safe for a install TorrentNG will manage long-term, but understand it before connecting to a client you don't want touched. See docs/TRACKER-IDENTITY.md in the TorrentNG repository. Never set the same Peer ID Override across more than one install -- a shared peer_id has gotten real users banned from private trackers for "running multiple clients."

Secret Key and API Tokens are required before first start regardless of backend -- they protect the WebUI and API themselves, not your torrent client. Generate both with e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`.

LOGIN: each install generates a unique WebUI password in its state directory. This template publishes on a public container bind, so first enter one of the configured API Tokens in either the Username or Password field. To retrieve the active login in Unraid, open the Docker tab, click the `torrentng-webui` container icon, choose Console, and run `torrentng auth-token` at the prompt. Use the printed WebUI username and password on TorrentNG's login page. The qBittorrent (or other backend) credentials in this template only let TorrentNG connect to that client; they are not the TorrentNG login. Then open Settings -> Security to choose a username and password of at least 16 characters. The API token remains available for automation. Tokens can also be set in `[auth].api_tokens` or `TNG_API_TOKENS` / `RTNG_API_TOKENS`. See the Unraid deployment README for details.

The image supports runtime PUID/PGID handling. This template defaults to Unraid's standard nobody:users identity, 99:100; the entrypoint prepares its small internal runtime directories and then drops to that non-root identity. Do not add an ExtraParams `--user` override: the entrypoint needs its short setup phase. Make sure the Downloads/Data and Appdata/State folders are owned by the selected PUID:PGID before first start. For the default paths shown below, use Unraid's Tools -> New Permissions on `/mnt/user/downloads` and `/mnt/user/appdata/torrentng-webui`, or run from the Unraid terminal: `chown -R 99:100 /mnt/user/downloads /mnt/user/appdata/torrentng-webui`. The container never recursively chowns mounted download trees at startup.";s:7:"Support";s:49:"https://github.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/issues";s:7:"Project";s:42:"https://github.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:92:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/main/templates/torrentng-webui.xml";s:6:"ReadMe";s:86:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/main/deploy/unraid/README.md";s:16:"ExtraSearchTerms";s:121:"rutorrent replacement webui bittorrent rtorrent qbittorrent transmission deluge prowlarr sonarr radarr autobrr cross-seed";s:8:"Requires";s:373:"An existing rTorrent, qBittorrent, Transmission, or Deluge install reachable on your network. Set Secret Key and API Tokens before first start; use an API Token in either WebUI login field. To retrieve the TorrentNG login, open Unraid's Docker tab, click this container's icon, choose Console, and run `torrentng auth-token`. Backend credentials do not log in to TorrentNG.";s:4:"Date";s:10:"2026-10-12";s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.9";s:7:"License";s:16:"GPL-3.0-or-later";s:6:"Config";a:25:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"WebUI/API Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"8080";s:7:"Default";s:4:"8080";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:124:"TorrentNG WebUI, REST/SSE/WebSocket API, and qBittorrent-compatible API (used by Prowlarr/Sonarr/Radarr/autobrr/cross-seed).";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Process UID (PUID)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PUID";s:7:"Default";s:2:"99";s:11:"Description";s:177:"Non-root Linux UID for the WebUI/API service. Defaults to Unraid's nobody UID 99. The entrypoint uses this value after its short setup phase; do not also set ExtraParams --user.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Process GID (PGID)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PGID";s:7:"Default";s:3:"100";s:11:"Description";s:157:"Non-root Linux GID for the WebUI/API service. Defaults to Unraid's users GID 100. Make the mounted Downloads/Data and Appdata paths accessible to this group.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:12:"Backend Type";s:6:"Target";s:11:"TNG_BACKEND";s:7:"Default";s:40:"rtorrent|qbittorrent|transmission|deluge";s:11:"Description";s:91:"Which existing torrent client this WebUI talks to. Fill in only the matching section below.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"Secret Key";s:6:"Target";s:14:"TNG_SECRET_KEY";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:101:"Random secret used to sign the WebUI's session cookies. Required. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"API Tokens";s:6:"Target";s:14:"TNG_API_TOKENS";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:170:"Comma-separated bearer token(s) for the REST/qBittorrent-compatible API (used by Prowlarr/Sonarr/Radarr/autobrr/cross-seed). Required. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:6;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"Downloads/Data";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Default";s:19:"/mnt/user/downloads";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:254:"Mount the SAME host folder your existing torrent client stores files in. TorrentNG reads this for file browsing and free-space reporting; it does not need to match exactly if your client only reports state remotely, but file-management API calls need it.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:7;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Appdata/State";s:6:"Target";s:18:"/var/lib/torrentng";s:7:"Default";s:39:"/mnt/user/appdata/torrentng-webui/state";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:199:"TorrentNG's own cache database and the generated per-install peer_id suffix (see docs/TRACKER-IDENTITY.md) live here. Back this up if you want the same identity to persist across container recreates.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:8;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"Config (optional)";s:6:"Target";s:7:"/config";s:7:"Default";s:40:"/mnt/user/appdata/torrentng-webui/config";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"ro";s:11:"Description";s:134:"Optional config.toml/rtorrent.rc overrides. Leave the folder empty to run entirely from the Variables below and the packaged defaults.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:9;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:27:"rTorrent: Managed (bundled)";s:6:"Target";s:20:"TNG_RTORRENT_MANAGED";s:7:"Default";s:1:"0";s:11:"Description";s:223:"0 (default): connect to an rTorrent you already run, using SCGI TCP Address or SCGI Socket Path below. 1: this container also launches and owns its own new rTorrent process (rarely wanted when fronting an existing install).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:10;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:26:"rTorrent: SCGI TCP Address";s:6:"Target";s:13:"TNG_SCGI_ADDR";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:491:"host:port of your existing rTorrent's SCGI listener, e.g. 192.168.1.10:5000. Requires that rTorrent to have network.scgi.open_port bound to a TCP address rather than a local Unix socket. Unlike the qBittorrent/Transmission/Deluge backends, get this right before starting: if the rTorrent backend can't reach and authenticate to rTorrent within about 15 seconds of container start, the container exits (Docker/Unraid will keep restarting and failing) instead of coming up in a degraded state.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:11;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:26:"rTorrent: SCGI Socket Path";s:6:"Target";s:15:"TNG_SCGI_SOCKET";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:240:"Alternative to SCGI TCP Address: container-side path to a Unix socket, for when you've mounted the host folder holding your existing rTorrent's socket file into this container yourself (add an extra Path in this template's Unraid settings).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:12;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:31:"rTorrent: Bundled Peer Port TCP";s:6:"Target";s:5:"50000";s:7:"Default";s:5:"50000";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:94:"Only used when rTorrent: Managed is set to 1. Ignored when connecting to an existing rTorrent.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:13;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:31:"rTorrent: Bundled Peer Port UDP";s:6:"Target";s:5:"50000";s:7:"Default";s:5:"50000";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"udp";s:11:"Description";s:94:"Only used when rTorrent: Managed is set to 1. Ignored when connecting to an existing rTorrent.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:14;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:20:"rTorrent: User-Agent";s:6:"Target";s:14:"TNG_USER_AGENT";s:7:"Default";s:24:"rtorrent/0.16.11/0.16.11";s:11:"Description";s:159:"Tracker-facing identity pushed to rTorrent on every start. Leave at the default unless you have a specific reason to change it -- see docs/TRACKER-IDENTITY.md.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:15;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:26:"rTorrent: Peer ID Override";s:6:"Target";s:11:"TNG_PEER_ID";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:346:"Leave empty (recommended): a unique 20-byte peer_id is generated and persisted per install automatically. Only set this to pin one specific, reproducible value -- e.g. a test fixture. NEVER copy the same value into more than one install/template: a shared peer_id has gotten real users banned from private trackers for 'running multiple clients'.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:16;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"qBittorrent: URL";s:6:"Target";s:19:"TNG_QBITTORRENT_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:69:"WebUI URL of your existing qBittorrent, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:8080";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:17;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:21:"qBittorrent: Username";s:6:"Target";s:24:"TNG_QBITTORRENT_USERNAME";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:27:"qBittorrent WebUI username.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:18;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:21:"qBittorrent: Password";s:6:"Target";s:24:"TNG_QBITTORRENT_PASSWORD";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:27:"qBittorrent WebUI password.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:19;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:20:"qBittorrent: No Auth";s:6:"Target";s:23:"TNG_QBITTORRENT_NO_AUTH";s:7:"Default";s:1:"0";s:11:"Description";s:182:"Set to 1 only if that qBittorrent's WebUI authentication is disabled for trusted-network access (its own 'Bypass authentication for clients on localhost/whitelisted subnet' setting).";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:20;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:17:"Transmission: URL";s:6:"Target";s:20:"TNG_TRANSMISSION_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:85:"RPC URL of your existing Transmission, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:9091/transmission/rpc";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:21;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:22:"Transmission: Username";s:6:"Target";s:25:"TNG_TRANSMISSION_USERNAME";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:26:"Transmission RPC username.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:22;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:22:"Transmission: Password";s:6:"Target";s:25:"TNG_TRANSMISSION_PASSWORD";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:26:"Transmission RPC password.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:23;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"Deluge: URL";s:6:"Target";s:14:"TNG_DELUGE_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:85:"JSON-RPC URL of your existing Deluge (deluge-web), e.g. http://192.168.1.10:8112/json";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:24;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:16:"Deluge: Password";s:6:"Target";s:19:"TNG_DELUGE_PASSWORD";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:23:"Deluge Web UI password.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:4:"true";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:49:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/templates/torrentng-webui.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:22:"torrentng's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:1:{i:0;s:11:"Downloaders";}s:16:"ChangeLogPresent";s:1:"1";s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1790295260;s:8:"Category";s:12:"Downloaders:";s:2:"ID";i:4584;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:9:"torrentng";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:15:"torrentng-webui";s:8:"RepoName";s:22:"torrentng's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:9:"torrentng";s:8:"SortName";s:15:"torrentng webui";s:6:"random";i:1322191476;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:4:"Path";s:119:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/torrentngsRepository/torrentng-torrentngwebui-latest.xml";s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:4585;a:38:{s:4:"Name";s:9:"torrentng";s:10:"Repository";s:41:"ghcr.io/snapetech/torrentng/native:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:72:"https://github.com/snapetech/TorrentNG/pkgs/container/torrentng%2Fnative";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:4:"Icon";s:71:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/main/icon.png";s:5:"WebUI";s:23:"http://[IP]:[PORT:8080]";s:8:"Overview";s:3566:"TorrentNG is a modern torrent management stack for headless power-user seeding at 10k-100k+ torrents and 200-500+ TB. This template runs torrentngd, TorrentNG's own first-party Rust BitTorrent client daemon, with its built-in WebUI, REST/SSE API, and qBittorrent-compatible API (for Prowlarr/Sonarr/Radarr/autobrr/cross-seed) all in one container. torrentngd owns torrent state, peer traffic, tracker state, storage, rechecks, jobs, and metrics directly -- it does not run or depend on rTorrent.

Use this template if you want a single self-contained TorrentNG install with no external torrent client. If you already run rTorrent, qBittorrent, Transmission, or Deluge and just want TorrentNG's WebUI/API in front of it instead, use the "TorrentNG WebUI" template.

FIRST RUN: torrentngd reads its settings from a config.toml file, not environment variables (this is intentional -- see the project's deployment docs). Before starting the container for the first time:
1. Create the appdata folder for Config below (e.g. /mnt/user/appdata/torrentng/config).
2. Either leave it empty to run with the packaged defaults (0.0.0.0:8080 WebUI, port 44444 for peer traffic, DHT on), or copy in a customized config.toml -- see deploy/native/config.toml in the TorrentNG repository for every field.
3. Set the "API Token" variable below to a random string; the container writes it to the path the packaged config already expects for authenticating the WebUI/API. Generate one with e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32` on any Linux box, or the Unraid terminal. This template publishes on a public container bind, so first login uses the API Token in either the Username or Password field. To retrieve the active login in Unraid, open the Docker tab, click the `torrentng` container icon, choose Console, and run `torrentngd auth-token` at the prompt. Use the printed WebUI username and password on TorrentNG's login page. Then open Settings -> Security to choose a username and password of at least 16 characters. The API token remains available for automation and can still be used in either login field. See the Unraid deployment README for details.

The image supports runtime PUID/PGID handling. This template defaults to Unraid's standard nobody:users identity, 99:100; the entrypoint prepares its small internal runtime directories and then drops to that non-root identity. Do not add an ExtraParams `--user` override: the entrypoint needs its short setup phase. Make sure the Data, Config, and State/DB appdata folders are owned by the selected PUID:PGID before first start. For the default paths shown below, use Unraid's Tools -> New Permissions on both `/mnt/user/downloads/torrentng` and `/mnt/user/appdata/torrentng`, or run from the Unraid terminal: `chown -R 99:100 /mnt/user/downloads/torrentng /mnt/user/appdata/torrentng`. The container never recursively chowns mounted download trees at startup.

RUNNING BEHIND A VPN: Runtime listen-port changes are supported through the authenticated native API and are persisted across restarts. For Gluetun, ProtonVPN NAT-PMP, and providers that assign a new forwarded port on reconnect, see the "Native torrentng behind Gluetun" section in the linked Unraid README or the deploy/native/compose.vpn.yml example. The native template can share an existing Gluetun namespace with Extra Parameters `--network=container:gluetun`; publish WebUI/API port 8080 on Gluetun, remove the fixed TorrentNG port mappings, and run the included `vpn-port-sync.sh` helper in that same network namespace using separate Gluetun and TorrentNG API keys.";s:7:"Support";s:49:"https://github.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/issues";s:7:"Project";s:42:"https://github.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:86:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/main/templates/torrentng.xml";s:6:"ReadMe";s:86:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/snapetech-org/TorrentNG/main/deploy/unraid/README.md";s:16:"ExtraSearchTerms";s:85:"bittorrent torrent client seedbox rtorrent qbittorrent transmission deluge torrentngd";s:8:"Requires";s:468:"Create the config appdata folder (optionally with a config.toml inside) and set an API token before first start. The install generates a unique WebUI password. To retrieve the active login, open Unraid's Docker tab, click this container's icon, choose Console, and run `torrentngd auth-token`. On this template's public bind, use the API token in either login field for first sign-in, then set a username and password of at least 16 characters in Settings -> Security.";s:4:"Date";s:10:"2026-10-12";s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.9";s:7:"License";s:16:"GPL-3.0-or-later";s:6:"Config";a:10:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:14:"WebUI/API Port";s:6:"Target";s:4:"8080";s:7:"Default";s:4:"8080";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:114:"TorrentNG WebUI, REST/SSE API, and qBittorrent-compatible API (used by Prowlarr/Sonarr/Radarr/autobrr/cross-seed).";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Peer Port TCP";s:6:"Target";s:5:"44444";s:7:"Default";s:5:"44444";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:169:"Incoming BitTorrent peer connections for direct bridge networking. For VPN-assigned ports, follow the Gluetun instructions in the Overview and remove this fixed mapping.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Peer Port UDP";s:6:"Target";s:5:"44444";s:7:"Default";s:5:"44444";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"udp";s:11:"Description";s:179:"Incoming BitTorrent peer connections (uTP/DHT) for direct bridge networking. For VPN-assigned ports, follow the Gluetun instructions in the Overview and remove this fixed mapping.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:3;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Process UID (PUID)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PUID";s:7:"Default";s:2:"99";s:11:"Description";s:166:"Non-root Linux UID for torrentngd. Defaults to Unraid's nobody UID 99. The entrypoint uses this value after its short setup phase; do not also set ExtraParams --user.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:4;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:8:{s:4:"Name";s:18:"Process GID (PGID)";s:6:"Target";s:4:"PGID";s:7:"Default";s:3:"100";s:11:"Description";s:143:"Non-root Linux GID for torrentngd. Defaults to Unraid's users GID 100. Make the mounted Data, Config, and State paths accessible to this group.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:5;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"Data";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Defau