e";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:39;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:5:"State";s:6:"Target";s:6:"/state";s:7:"Default";s:26:"/mnt/user/appdata/plungarr";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:57:"Persists first-seen ages and loop guards across restarts.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:40;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:19:"Completed downloads";s:6:"Target";s:10:"/downloads";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:193:"Optional. SABnzbd's completed folder (the one holding tv/, movies/, ...). Needed only for the leftover-folder sweep; set Leftover dirs too. Read-only is enough unless Leftover action is delete.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:41;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:13:"Leftover dirs";s:6:"Target";s:13:"LEFTOVER_DIRS";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:238:"Container paths whose children are job folders, e.g. /downloads/tv,/downloads/movies. Empty keeps the leftover sweep off. SABnzbd never deletes completed job folders and an arr only cleans up its own, so folders nobody references pile up.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:42;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:15:"Leftover action";s:6:"Target";s:15:"LEFTOVER_ACTION";s:7:"Default";s:6:"notify";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:174:"notify (report each unreferenced old folder in the digest) or delete (remove it; needs the path mapped read-write). Only direct children of the leftover dirs, never symlinks.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}i:43;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:26:"Leftover minimum age hours";s:6:"Target";s:22:"LEFTOVER_MIN_AGE_HOURS";s:7:"Default";s:2:"24";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:92:"A folder is a leftover only if neither it nor any file in it changed within this many hours.";s:4:"Type";s:8:"Variable";s:7:"Display";s:8:"advanced";s:8:"Required";s:5:"false";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:0:"";}}s:12:"TemplatePath";s:42:"/tmp/GitHub/AppFeed/templates/plungarr.xml";s:4:"Repo";s:24:"stove-bison's Repository";s:12:"CategoryList";a:2:{i:0;s:11:"Downloaders";i:1;s:15:"Tools-Utilities";}s:16:"ChangeLogPresent";s:1:"1";s:9:"FirstSeen";i:1788797528;s:8:"Category";s:28:"Downloaders: Tools:Utilities";s:2:"ID";i:4656;s:11:"Displayable";b:1;s:6:"Author";s:11:"stove-bison";s:13:"DockerHubName";s:8:"plungarr";s:8:"RepoName";s:24:"stove-bison's Repository";s:10:"SortAuthor";s:11:"stove-bison";s:8:"SortName";s:8:"plungarr";s:6:"random";i:184657996;s:9:"Blacklist";b:0;s:6:"MinVer";s:3:"6.0";s:4:"Path";s:115:"/tmp/community.applications/tempFiles/templates-community-apps/stovebisonsRepository/stovebison-plungarr-latest.xml";s:10:"Deprecated";b:0;s:10:"Compatible";b:1;s:16:"ModeratorComment";N;}i:4657;a:37:{s:4:"Name";s:11:"mybabynotes";s:10:"Repository";s:42:"ghcr.io/straplocked/mybabynotes-aio:latest";s:8:"Registry";s:73:"https://github.com/straplocked/mybabynotes/pkgs/container/mybabynotes-aio";s:7:"Network";s:6:"bridge";s:5:"Shell";s:2:"sh";s:10:"Privileged";s:5:"false";s:7:"Support";s:49:"https://github.com/straplocked/mybabynotes/issues";s:7:"Project";s:42:"https://github.com/straplocked/mybabynotes";s:6:"ReadMe";s:49:"https://github.com/straplocked/mybabynotes#readme";s:8:"Overview";s:1583:"Baby tracker built for one household sharing its babies' log — up to six adults (parent and caregiver roles) and up to ten children — with your data in one SQLite file on your server.

Three taps from pocket to logged: the entry sheet opens pre-stamped with the current time and a prediction of what you're about to log (alternating nursing sides, last bottle amount, feed-vs-diaper rhythm). Entries write locally first and sync when there's signal, so 3am logging never waits on the network.

The whole household, one log: everyone joins by invite — as a parent (full control) or a caregiver (logs, timers, and shifts) — and sees the same log live over websockets. Shift handoffs are a first-class flow — "I need to sleep, take him" becomes a request with a note, an auto-drafted plan from the baby's rhythm, and a handback summary instead of a "when did you…" conversation.

Installable PWA (Add to Home Screen), Now/History views with since-cards, totals, 7-day charts, sleep and nursing timers, and optional web-push reminders — no cloud account, no FCM, no telemetry.

First boot generates all secrets into /data/.env automatically; the first account registered claims the instance and invites the rest of the household. Everything (database + secrets) lives in the one /data share — back that folder up and you've backed up the app.

For access beyond your LAN (and for installing the PWA + push notifications, which need HTTPS), put a reverse proxy with websocket support in front — Nginx Proxy Manager or SWAG both work; enable websockets for the /app path.";s:5:"WebUI";s:21:"http://[IP]:[PORT:80]";s:11:"TemplateURL";s:92:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/straplocked/mybabynotes/main/deploy/unraid/ca-template.xml";s:4:"Icon";s:88:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/straplocked/mybabynotes/main/public/icons/icon-512.png";s:8:"Requires";s:128:"Remote access needs an HTTPS reverse proxy with websocket support (the PWA install prompt and push notifications require HTTPS).";s:6:"Config";a:5:{i:0;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:10:"WebUI Port";s:6:"Target";s:2:"80";s:7:"Default";s:4:"3500";s:4:"Mode";s:3:"tcp";s:11:"Description";s:99:"Host port for the web app. The container serves everything (app, API, websockets) on this one port.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Port";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:4:"3500";}i:1;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:4:"Data";s:6:"Target";s:5:"/data";s:7:"Default";s:29:"/mnt/user/appdata/mybabynotes";s:4:"Mode";s:2:"rw";s:11:"Description";s:392:"All state: the SQLite database and the auto-generated .env with this instance's secrets. Back this folder up. NOTE: if you previously ran the docker-compose install, do NOT point this at that folder — its database sits one level down in data/, so the container would start on an empty database and leave your log behind. See docs/operations.md, 'Moving a compose install to the Unraid app'.";s:4:"Type";s:4:"Path";s:7:"Display";s:6:"always";s:8:"Required";s:4:"true";s:4:"Mask";s:5:"false";}s:5:"value";s:29:"/mnt/user/appdata/mybabynotes";}i:2;a:2:{s:11:"@attributes";a:9:{s:4:"Name";s:7:"APP_URL";s:6:"Target";s:7:"APP_URL";s:7:"Default";s:0:"";s:4:"Mode";s:0:"";s:11:"Description";s:191:"Optional: your public https origin (e.g. https://notes.example.com) when running behind a